  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia (en): Cornus mas § Cultivation",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cornus_mas#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
        "revision": 1371113483,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "The shrub was not native to the British Isles. William Turner had only heard of the plant in 1548, but by 1551 he had heard of one at Hampton Court Palace. Gerard said it was to be found in the gardens \"of such as love rare and dainty plants\".\nThe appreciation of the early acid-yellow flowers is largely a 20th-century development.\n\n■ Cultivars\nThe following cultivars have gained the Royal Horticultural Society's Award of Garden Merit (confirmed 2017): \n\n'Aurea' (yellow leaves and flowers, red fruit)\n'Golden Glory' (profuse yellow flowers, shiny red berries)\n'Variegata' (variegated leaves, glossy red fruit)",
        "truncated": false
      },
      "fr": {
        "source": "Wikipedia (fr): Cornus mas § Culture",
        "source_url": "https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cornus_mas#Culture",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
        "revision": 239278554,
        "heading": "Culture",
        "text": "Le Cornouiller mâle apprécie les sols frais et calcaires. Selon une autre source, il préfère les sols secs et bien drainés. \nIl supporte bien la taille et peut être formé en haie. La fructification est sujette à l'alternance. Il se propage habituellement par marcottage ou bouture à talon en automne. Le semis peut nécessiter deux ans avant de lever puis 8 à 10 ans avant de fructifier, et ne  garantit pas la fidélité au fruit d'origine.\nLes semis de noyaux donnent les plus beaux sujets.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
